import json
p = "/home/hatch/workspace/apex-audit/docs/university/data/courses/_il29-build/m27.json"
d = json.load(open(p))
d["objectives"] = [
 "Write an adversarial threat model (goals, capabilities, knowledge) for an ML-based detector.",
 "Execute a worked evasion attack against a linear classifier and compute the minimum perturbation.",
 "Distinguish poisoning from evasion by phase, evidence, and response.",
 "Evaluate defenses (adversarial training, sanitization, ensembles, certified robustness) against adaptive attackers.",
 "Describe prompt injection, jailbreaks, and tool-use exfiltration against LLM security tooling.",
 "Plan defense rotation under the arms-race mindset and compose ML verdicts with independent deterministic controls so no model output is load-bearing.",
]
# M27 has 6 notes: indices 0..5 -> threat model, evasion, poisoning-vs-evasion, LLM tooling, arms race, model-says-safe
exts = {
 0: "One more use of the threat model that teams miss: it scopes your red team. A red-team exercise without a threat model tests whatever the red team felt like trying; a red-team exercise driven by the threat model tests the attacker you actually face. Hand the red team your written threat model — goals, capabilities, knowledge — and ask them to play exactly that adversary, including staying within the stated query budget and perturbation constraints. If they break the system within the model, the defense is insufficient; if they can only break it by exceeding the model (say, needing white-box access you do not expose), you have learned where your assumptions carry weight — and which assumptions to monitor. Revisit the threat model on a schedule, because attackers' capabilities only grow: query budgets get cheaper, substitute models get better, and last year's 'unrealistic' attacker is next year's script kiddie. The threat model is a living document, and the red team's job includes telling you when it is obsolete.",
 1: "Two extensions worth making explicit. First, targeted vs untargeted: our example flipped positive to negative (untargeted — any wrong class will do). Targeted evasion aims for a specific wrong class ('classify this malware as a known-benign system file'), which is harder but far more useful to attackers — and the optimization just aims the push at the target class's region instead of merely away from the true one. Second, transferability, the property that makes black-box evasion practical: adversarial examples crafted against one model often fool other models trained on similar data, because models learn similar boundaries. The attacker needs no access to your model — they train their own substitute, craft white-box attacks against it, and fire them at you. Defenses that rely on 'the attacker doesn't know our architecture' die here. The operational takeaway: evaluate your detector against transferred attacks from public substitute models, not just against attacks crafted on your own weights. If a perturbation built on an open-source model fools your production detector, your threat model must assume black-box attackers succeed — because they just did.",
 2: "Now the composition problem, because real attackers do not pick one phase. Consider the full campaign: poison the training data with a backdoor (phase one), then at inference time use tiny trigger-carrying inputs that evade the poisoned model's normal behavior (phase two) — or conversely, use evasion probes to map the model's boundary, then poison the retraining pipeline with samples just inside it. Your defenses must therefore be phase-aware but not phase-blind: input sanitization will not catch a backdoor (the trigger is a legitimate input), and data provenance will not catch evasion (the training data was clean). The diagnostic question when a model misbehaves is always 'which phase?' — and the evidence differs: poisoning shows up as misbehavior correlated with specific training-data slices or trigger patterns, reproducible across inputs carrying the trigger; evasion shows up as sensitivity to small perturbations of otherwise-normal inputs, with clean training data. Build your incident runbook for 'model misbehaves' with both branches, because the first hour of response determines whether you do data surgery or input hardening — and doing the wrong one wastes the hour while the attacker keeps working.",
 4: "What, if anything, ends the race? Two candidates, honestly assessed. Formal verification and certified robustness exit the cycle by replacing empirical hope with proof — but they currently cover small models, small perturbation radii, and narrow threat models. Promising, not yet operational for frontier-scale systems. The other candidate is economics: raise the attacker's cost above the value of success. Every defense in the toolkit does this partially — adversarial training forces larger perturbations, rate limiting forces slower extraction, ensembles force beating multiple models. None is a wall; each is a tax. The operator's job is to set the total tax above the attacker's budget for your specific assets — which is why threat modeling comes first: you cannot price the tax without knowing the attacker's budget. And note the asymmetry that favors you: the defender deploys once and protects many decisions; the attacker must re-win every evasion. Invest in defenses with this asymmetry — architectural controls like allowlisting that the attacker must defeat per-action, not per-model. The race does not end, but you can make your stretch of it unprofitable.",
 5: "Design the human's role for the adversarial setting specifically. Normal human-in-the-loop assumes the human catches the model's mistakes; adversarial human-in-the-loop must assume the model's mistakes are crafted to fool the human too — confidently wrong outputs with plausible reasoning. So the verification interface matters enormously: show the human the evidence, not just the verdict ('here are the three signals, here is what each says, here is where they disagree'), because a human shown only 'malicious, 97% confident' has nothing to verify with. Require the human to state their own verdict before seeing the model's — otherwise anchoring bias makes the human a rubber stamp for the machine. And sample-verify even the agreements: periodically have analysts re-examine cases where human and model agreed, because correlated failures (both wrong the same way) are invisible otherwise. The composed system — model as sensor, deterministic policy as gate, human as judge with independent evidence — is more expensive than 'the model says safe.' It is also the only arrangement that survives contact with an adaptive adversary, which is the only kind this module has been about.",
}
for i, e in exts.items():
    d["notes"][i]["body"] = d["notes"][i]["body"] + "\n\n" + e
for n in d["notes"]:
    assert len(n["body"].split()) >= 400, (n["title"], len(n["body"].split()))
assert 4 <= len(d["objectives"]) <= 6
with open(p, "w") as f: json.dump(d, f, ensure_ascii=False)
print("m27 ok:", [len(n["body"].split()) for n in d["notes"]])
